  "items": [
    {
      "id": "lem-nrqm-many-body-one-body-reduction-trace-law",
      "raw_sha256": "3c31c2bcf2a0efeb201cfc842dacc9110482502b7a66b5ee65146461febf47a8",
      "review": "Checked every proof step and exact finite partial-trace, bounded tensor-map, density and trace clauses. Sector density zero-extension remains invariant because the antisymmetric signs cancel. Transported slot permutations with hat-pi(1)=i give the same one-slot readout, independent of choice. Finite partial trace is positive trace-one; summing N identical slot expectations gives the stated N factor. N=1 uses identity, and a zero sector admits no density."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-noninteracting-sector-diagonalization",
      "raw_sha256": "60fe1c7ba06f0eb8b45353730fd60729561c78fb8b05fecbd616c9a57a25c56d",
      "review": "Checked every proof step against finite spectral theory, occupation basis/counts, tensor maps and stationary-state readings. Energy acts by the sum of mode eigenvalues on the repaired occupation vectors; normalize the symmetric orthogonal basis for traces. Repeated eigenvalues retain configuration multiplicities. Negative energies are allowed and the two Boltzmann bounds have the correct extrema. N>d fermions give zero sector/Z=0 and no ground eigenvalue or density. Physical conclusions stay conditional on W=0 and finite declared modes."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-one-body-readout-law",
      "raw_sha256": "e5cf4b20befff4e6e936942b15e8d4a67be4994ad697bca0bd3e249df5959abb",
      "review": "Checked all steps, partial-trace source, rank-one calculus and corrected Slater formula with first-variable-linear pairing. Nonidentity permutations move at least two slots, so distinct orthonormal orbitals kill the off-diagonal one-slot pairings. The reduced density is the equal rank-one mixture, hence the readout sum. N=1 is defined locally as Psi=u1 and checked directly rather than invoking the n>=2 Slater clause. Bounded readouts and conditional apparatus identification are retained."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-exchange-energy-splitting",
      "raw_sha256": "25af5432d80bc53f4c341bccc2e8649d9ab43c0f4448fa3d36244a1342c0ac0b",
      "review": "Checked every step, Slater direct/exchange hypotheses, product-L2 identification, finite-spin regrouping, bounded spatial and Coulomb domains. Combined spin/spatial vectors are normalized and antisymmetric; opposite singlet/triplet parity selects spatial signs. Orthogonality kills one-body cross terms. H2 product orbitals have the required mixed weak derivatives; even bounded W makes D and X real. Coulomb uses the actual translated Hardy/Gaussian argument with L1 overlap, not an assumed L2 overlap. Physical kinetic normalization is realized by the specified mass substitution; bounded external addition uses Kato-Rellich. No unqualified spectral or laboratory assertion is inferred."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-thermal-equilibrium-characterization",
      "raw_sha256": "bdb418cfdda58e04a557464b98a61e4e8ca65d59ef5efa874f0e16944f9c0a9e",
      "review": "Checked every comparison/equality step and full finite Gibbs proof, strict logarithm inequality and entropy/Gibbs definitions. The model sector is nonzero finite dimensional. Gibbs eigenweights are strictly positive; zero competitor weights contribute the positive d(0,r) term. The overlap matrix is bistochastic; the sum of nonnegative scalar discrepancies establishes the bound. Equality forces matching positive weights along every nonzero overlap, hence equality of operators even with degenerate energies. The Gibbs reverse equality is computed directly. No spatial/infinite-dimensional entropy or thermalization claim is made."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-thermal-occupation-predictions",
      "raw_sha256": "2a5a0be106d4c3c14da00a171ca1b7bc4a97176145a577bac34c0af6d67d234e",
      "review": "Checked every step and full single-mode thermal, signed fermion/dilute and countable product proofs, diagonal graph-domain lemma, number-operator domains and moment readings. Bosonic geometric moments converge for positive energy. The two-state fermionic computation directly covers arbitrary real epsilon1 and mu. Countable effective G=H-mu N has the displayed squared-energy diagonal domain/core, not a formal unbounded subtraction; summability gives only finitely many negative shifted fermion energies and a lower bound. Bose requires mu<inf epsilon plus summability. Products are full number-variable ensembles rather than fixed-N traces. Signed dilute deviations are Bose positive and Fermi negative, with the stated absolute bounds. Gibbs assignment and dimensionless count readouts remain adopted model assumptions."
    },
    {
      "id": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-unitary-evolution-preserves-entropy",
      "raw_sha256": "fab91f5c193f6691c80cb8e0df66dade38ee00d9556a0c92fd195c90848f178d",
      "review": "Checked every step against finite spectral, unitary flow, entropy and canonical-ensemble definitions. Unitary conjugation preserves the complete eigenvalue multiset including zeros; Gibbs density commutes with the same energy flow. Applying the inverse flow excludes exact production from a different preparation. Ensemble existence excludes the zero-sector Gibbs branch. The result says nothing about readout limits, open systems or empirical relaxation."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-nrqm-many-body-two-site-bond-thermal-ensemble",
      "raw_sha256": "f67f336de2f3f7a0857489b745b15c4015106247d1a8c51c10810e59a4d52997",
      "review": "Checked all algebraic verification steps and full two-level thermal lemma, rank-one calculus and ensemble/entropy readings. The bond matrix has eigenvalues -t,+t and the normalized +/- eigenvectors. Scalar shift multiplies partition trace by exp(beta t), leaves density unchanged and shifts free energy by -t. Mean is -t tanh(beta t); beta,t>0 give strict bounds and nonmaximal entropy. No laboratory splitting is claimed."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-nrqm-many-body-finite-mode-noninteracting-thermal-ensemble",
      "raw_sha256": "0b2e9af97ed9c8a8372fe58c24c5359dcaca00628cc46ba7a2514dbb945c86c1",
      "review": "Checked every verification step with finite occupation eigenvalues/counts and adopted Gibbs normalization. For three fermion configurations the energies are epsilon,2epsilon,3epsilon; bosonic two-mode configurations have 0,epsilon,2epsilon. Positive finite weights normalize on the nonzero sectors, occupation expectations sum to N=2, and the quoted energy/partition formulas follow. Finite-mode and preparation qualifications are preserved."
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-nrqm-many-body-gas-statistics-dilute-limits",
      "raw_sha256": "ed90add84cc1c47d256665fd40127299bdb56c7ac3428ce427ce45b6f18d2883",
      "review": "Checked every verification step and full signed dilute/product suppliers, thermal prediction clauses and geometric-series starting index. At q=1/10 the occupations are 1/9 and 1/11, signed deviations +1/90 and -1/110. Linear positive mode energies give a convergent geometric sum; constant energies give divergence. The Bose chemical-potential condition is retained. These exact model examples establish no continuum gas, thermodynamic limit or measured statistic."
    },
    {
      "id": "texp-nrqm-many-body-two-site-no-thermalization",
      "raw_sha256": "6f82d50cfa6084bfee1f230cd608921eb29902267ebd76c2e8ea9a2ed70d647b",
      "review": "Checked every hypothetical derivation step using the full bond example, Euler/parity identities and unitary-entropy theorem. Flow gives cos(theta)|1>+i sin(theta)|2>, hence the site probability cos squared. Gibbs site probability is 1/2 and mean energy is negative, whereas the chosen initial pure state has mean zero. Stationarity plus inverse flow excludes exact equality with Gibbs at any time. No observation is invented and no conclusion about open-system thermalization follows."
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-nrqm-many-body-finite-spin-product-identification",
      "raw_sha256": "b92d379ddbdae510630d98d5117131be5f9b313a54b1b713a6b753fab925a3c3",
      "review": "Checked every construction/domain step with component wavefunction and Euclidean product unitaries, finite tensor maps and general restriction theorem (a)-(d). Product isometry extends onto by dense product/simple components. Transported slot action permutes coordinates and internal indices consistently. Componentwise H2 domains and finite adjoints persist under constant spin permutations; equal masses and even potential give commutation and reducing projections. Uses the general CC restriction result, not its separate AC scalar application. N=1, r=1 and zero-sector operator branches are explicit."
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-nrqm-many-body-fixed-n-countable-mode-partition-criterion",
      "raw_sha256": "aab48bdd064dbadbb5cacb4426e24b0aac2b99f35403b8a75b22a3aa35b9573f",
      "review": "Checked every basis/domain/trace step with occupation basis, diagonal adjoint/core, Parseval, PVM and nuclear-series sources. Compatible finite-prefix bases exhaust the supplied countable tensor basis. Squared-energy domain gives self-adjoint closure and finite occupation graph core; lower bound Nb makes exponential bounded. Extended positive diagonal trace is independent by nonnegative finite-subsupremum interchange and Parseval. Finite sums yield norm-convergent nuclear approximation; the converse uses ordinary positive trace. Fixed {1,...,N-1,j} configurations give the lower tail bound and N=1 uses empty product 1; upper bound is the ordered-tuple sum. Thus both statistics have the exact fixed-N iff, without substituting full Fock trace."
    }
